Subject: The profile holds no reference on an unhashed shape, and drops the records of bytecode on the no-trace list

What this does
  - `js_aot_prof_pic` and `js_aot_prof_reg` record a receiver shape (with a reference) only if it is hashed. An unhashed
    shape is counted as a fill and not recorded, as for a fifth shape; a pc whose receiver shape is unhashed becomes
    polymorphic.
  - `js_aot_prof_dump` drops the shape references of the site and pc records of the bytecode on `gc_notrace_list` as
    well as `gc_obj_list`.

Why we need it
  An unhashed shape is owned by one object and `add_property` appends to it in place. A reference held by the profile made
  that add assert (`JS_REF_COUNT(p->shape) == 1`), and in a build without asserts it changed the shape the record names.
  A hashed shape is copy-on-write, so a held reference cannot be mutated under the record. Bytecode on the no-trace list
  kept its recorded shapes after the dump; a shape holds its prototype, which holds the function and so the bytecode, a
  cycle through an edge the collector does not trace, so the final collection could not free the realm and
  `JS_FreeRuntime` asserted on a non-empty object list.

Functions touched
  `js_aot_prof_pic`, `js_aot_prof_reg`, `js_aot_prof_dump`.

No.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -63143,0 +63144,7 @@
+    /* An unhashed shape is owned by its one object and is mutated in place when a property is
+       added; a hashed shape is copy-on-write (js_shape_prepare_update and add_property clone it
+       while it has a second reference). A reference held here on an unhashed shape would make
+       that add assert, or, without asserts, change the shape the record names. So only a hashed
+       shape is recorded; the fill is still counted, as for a fifth shape. */
+    if (!shape->is_hashed)
+        return;
